[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Let go of your expectations and just look at&nbsp; me wearing these. These are Snap Specs. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp; these do not look like my everyday glasses,&nbsp; but yet they\'re not really trying to be,&nbsp;&nbsp; are they? Well, that\'s the question. Snap has&nbsp; this pair of augmented reality glasses that&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I finally am getting to try that\'s going to be&nbsp; available for $2,000 that they\'re big. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp; you look at this and they\'re big glasses, but what&nbsp; they do inside is more advanced than any pair of&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'smart glasses that I\'ve seen before. And that\'s&nbsp; where these are landing. Kind of in a territory&nbsp;&nbsp; that doesn\'t really exist yet. And yet, I\'m trying&nbsp; them. Let me explain what I\'m talking about. Snap&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'brought me out here to LA to check out the newest&nbsp; version of specs and to actually try them for&nbsp;&nbsp; myself. And I was super curious to do it because&nbsp; I\'ve seen just about everything else out there. I&nbsp;&nbsp; wanted to see how these new specs compare. So,&nbsp; why am I kind of jazzed about this? Because I&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'followed this territory of AR glasses for years.&nbsp; Maybe we\'re walking around and there\'s this&nbsp;&nbsp; whole world of 3D things around you that you\'re&nbsp; interacting with. Do you even want to do that?&nbsp;&nbsp; Is that something you want to do in your life?&nbsp; We\'ll get to that in a second. But the point is,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is it capable? There have only been a few devices&nbsp; that have even shown 3D things going into the real&nbsp;&nbsp; world in something that you can wear around all&nbsp; the time. Magic Leap was one of them. Had a little&nbsp;&nbsp; Snap-On processor pack. Snap has been making AR&nbsp; glasses that are in this type of idea for a while&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in developer modes and Snap Specs are pushing that&nbsp; out with a promised longer battery life and the&nbsp;&nbsp; ability to blend AI, mixed reality, handtracking&nbsp; and all of that into a package with apps that fits&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on your face. And by the way, Kim can can also&nbsp; connect with cellular with an optional charging&nbsp;&nbsp; pack glasses case that will have Verizon service&nbsp; and other services in other compatible countries.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'If you\'ve ever used Apple Vision Pro, if you\'ve&nbsp; ever used Samsung Galaxy XR, if you\'ve ever used&nbsp;&nbsp; MetaQuest 3 and 3S with their mixed reality&nbsp; features, you\'ve seen things that blend the&nbsp;&nbsp; real world and virtual stuff with pass through&nbsp; cameras. This is doing it with actual transparent&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'lenses in the real world. As you can see, there\'s&nbsp; a there\'s a rainbow effect on the lens. This is&nbsp;&nbsp; using wave guides. It doesn\'t look as as clear&nbsp; and transparent as everyday glasses. There\'s&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'also built-in transition sunglass lenses into&nbsp; this. So, if I step outside for a while, you may&nbsp;&nbsp; already see that it\'s starting to tint, but what&nbsp; I can see when I interact with it is this world&nbsp;&nbsp; that appears in front of me when I turn them on.&nbsp; And the viewing area is about this big. That may&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'not sound tremendously large, but it\'s larger than&nbsp; what Snap has done before in these AR glasses. And&nbsp;&nbsp; the only time I\'ve seen larger than that in AR&nbsp; glasses is when they\'ve used entirely different&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'technologies. Glasses like XRI and Beecher use&nbsp; bird bath displays, if you know what that is,&nbsp;&nbsp; that reflect these prism lenses down. They get in&nbsp; the way. They\'re they\'re they\'re not transparent&nbsp;&nbsp; like this. So, the point being, I haven\'t really&nbsp; seen AR glasses that are doing this in a field&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of view with something that actually works like&nbsp; this. That area is actually large enough to be&nbsp;&nbsp; able to see what looks like a pretty nicely sized&nbsp; screen floating in front of me that I can actually&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'drag around with my fingers with this cursor&nbsp; and move around or it could follow you. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp; there are a lot of different apps that Snap is&nbsp; promising on specs and I got to try some of them.&nbsp;&nbsp; Uh some of them are like games like dominoes or&nbsp; there\'s a web browser so you could watch YouTube&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'or you could use Disney Plus or you could you know&nbsp; watch movies on it. Try Google Docs which I tried&nbsp;&nbsp; and connected with a keyboard. Would I do work&nbsp; on this? The response time with the keyboard was&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a little slow. Um but it was just showing that you&nbsp; potentially could if you wanted to and the display&nbsp;&nbsp; is reasonably crisp and bright. Meaning reasonably&nbsp; in that in bright daylight it might get washed&nbsp;&nbsp; out. There\'s a little bit of color variation that&nbsp; comes with waveguides compared to other types of&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'display technologies, but very usable. And the&nbsp; coolest stuff I got to see was when it began to&nbsp;&nbsp; blend the tracking with the real world. I tried&nbsp; a soccer training game that allowed me to set&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'up a play area by mapping out a a little space,&nbsp; recognizing the ground. I set up the ball and I&nbsp;&nbsp; started doing dribbling practice and boom, it knew&nbsp; where the ball was going to go and then I kicked&nbsp;&nbsp; to the next part or I mean it sense where the ball&nbsp; was going to go. When I got there it went to the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'next part. There\'s golf training games. There\'s&nbsp; aspirations to do things like art or you know&nbsp;&nbsp; learning piano things that you may have seen on&nbsp; phones with AR in the past on things like vision&nbsp;&nbsp; pro netquest but in something relatively much&nbsp; smaller. And the relatively is the part I want&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to stress here because no, these do not look like&nbsp; everyday glasses that I would be wearing out with&nbsp;&nbsp; somebody. I think it\'s really interesting though&nbsp; to think about these to experience stuff in the&nbsp;&nbsp; real world for periods of time to enable outdoor&nbsp; experiences or multiplayer things. Maybe you\'d see&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it at a theme park. Maybe you\'d see it at a museum&nbsp; and put it on. By the way, how do these feel?&nbsp;&nbsp; Chunky, but actually resting pretty comfortably&nbsp; on my face. I feel some pressure on the temples&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'because, as you can see, they don\'t really rest&nbsp; so much on my ears as much as they kind of press&nbsp;&nbsp; here. But they feel very solidly designed. They&nbsp; feel sleek and pretty sturdy. I wouldn\'t want to&nbsp;&nbsp; wear this against my face for too too long, I&nbsp; don\'t think. But I feel like I would wear this&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for half an hour or even who knows, maybe an hour,&nbsp; but then I\'d want to maybe take a break. These can&nbsp;&nbsp; support my prescription using prescription inserts&nbsp; that you snap into the snap specs. Um, so it\'s not&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the same as getting a full lens put in, but good&nbsp; enough for using if you need it. Right now, I can&nbsp;&nbsp; see things around me perfectly fine, even though&nbsp; it\'s not technically my exact prescription. I have&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a high tolerance for lots of strange things on my&nbsp; face. I mean, I\'m wearing big, heavy VR headsets,&nbsp;&nbsp; small VR headsets, mixed reality things, smart&nbsp; glasses. I\'ve tried a lot of them. I actually&nbsp;&nbsp; feel like this is pretty easy to put on and use.&nbsp; And you interact with it by staring at your hand.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And it brings up a little menu. And then I can&nbsp; tap little things with my finger to bring up the&nbsp;&nbsp; hot button for the home screen. And then once I do&nbsp; that, I can then reach out at a distance and move&nbsp;&nbsp; the cursor with kind of aiming force powers to&nbsp; pinch. It could also do direct touch to objects.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Um you could pinch things directly or pinch to&nbsp; zoom or have things that you would interact with.&nbsp;&nbsp; Uh, and there are already a handful of apps.&nbsp; There\'s, uh, navigation for showing you, uh,&nbsp;&nbsp; real world directions, including a map that would&nbsp; sprout in 3D in front of me that I could look at.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I tried translation that showed captions that&nbsp; were coming between me and the other person. I&nbsp;&nbsp; uh, got to try multiplayer because when you&nbsp; connect with another person wearing specs,&nbsp;&nbsp; you can look at them and then begin an experience&nbsp; that you both share. So we started playing&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'dominoes together on a table or you could share a&nbsp; 3D experience or whatever and that could be done&nbsp;&nbsp; in larger groups too so that everybody could&nbsp; jump into one experience communally. And since&nbsp;&nbsp; there is no other extending uh processor pack&nbsp; this is it battery life is promised at up to 4&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hours I\'ve already noticed the battery life&nbsp; has started to go down significantly even in&nbsp;&nbsp; my shorter demos. So I\'m sure you will probably&nbsp; need to recharge for some of these things sooner&nbsp;&nbsp; than later. But I think of this as kind of a short&nbsp; experience thing, uh, beginning of an exploration,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a testing of where things can go. I mean, look,&nbsp; you\'ve got Samsung and Google promising AR glasses&nbsp;&nbsp; in the future. Apple is supposed to be coming&nbsp; out with not just smart glasses, but something&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that might be a shrunken down version of Vision&nbsp; Pro in AR glasses form. Meta has already talked&nbsp;&nbsp; about making AR glasses for years. And it looks&nbsp; like they may start bringing the VR and the smart&nbsp;&nbsp; glasses together. And what Snap\'s doing is getting&nbsp; ahead of that and exploring some of these things&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that I think others are going to explore too.&nbsp; They\'re also getting ahead of something involving&nbsp;&nbsp; AI because they\'re launching an AI app called&nbsp; Snap Intelligence that\'s going to run on Mac&nbsp;&nbsp; and iPhone that will try to be an AI assistant.&nbsp; This is also going to run on the glasses. And&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that\'s a lot of what Snap is trying to do here is&nbsp; run AI agents, show ways you could use AI, show&nbsp;&nbsp; ways you can make apps for this. How will that&nbsp; all work? How will Snap intelligence function?&nbsp;&nbsp; I don\'t know yet, but I\'m really curious to try it&nbsp; because I\'ve seen a lot of AI infused tech coming&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and not a lot that are truly open to working with&nbsp; all platforms. Something that Snap seems like they&nbsp;&nbsp; want to do. Those are all my thoughts on Snap&nbsp; specs right now. I\'m sure I\'m going to have a&nbsp;&nbsp; lot more. This is them on my face. They\'re coming&nbsp; out later this fall and Snap\'s going to have a&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'place to demo them in Los Angeles and I hope&nbsp; they\'re going to have more places to demo them&nbsp;&nbsp; and I hope they start putting them in little&nbsp; experiences that you could try them because I&nbsp;&nbsp; actually think that\'ll be the best way to use it&nbsp; as opposed to actually buying a pair for yourself.&nbsp;&nbsp; So, what do you think? Absurd? Fascinating? All&nbsp; of the above? Uh, let us know in the comments.'
